-- Row 99 wave 1: a definition may omit its signature, and the judgement is
-- inferred. The signature line is the single largest thing generation 1 spends
-- on ceremony — doc 97 §4 puts it at about 8.7% of the corpus's lines — and it
-- is deleted first because it is the only deletion with an answer key:
-- generation 1's hand-written signatures are still in every other file, so
-- `voltaic sig --verify` can check inferred-against-written before anything is
-- removed. 1,165 of 1,165 agree, which is the licence for this file.
--
-- What makes these inferable is that each one reaches a *written* type: `scaled`
-- is annotated, so `doubled` learns `u64 -> u64` from the call alone. An
-- unsigned definition that reaches no written type anywhere is not inferable,
-- and that is not a gap — rustc needs an annotation to break the same cycle.
scaled : u64 -> u64
scaled n = n * 2

doubled n = scaled n

quadrupled n = doubled (doubled n)

main : ()
main =
  println! \-> "#{(quadrupled 3)}"
  ()
